-- title:
--   Lower bound for the discrete kernel at mesh $h$ by cone-restricted cube volumes
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$. Write $\lVert u\rVert_\infty = \sup_i |u_i|$, let $A_h(u) = \{s : \lVert s - u\rVert_\infty < h/2\}$ be the open cube of side $h$ centred at $u$, and let $h\mathbb{Z}^d = \{x : \text{each } x_i \in h\mathbb{Z}\}$ be the scaled lattice. For a kernel $k$ on $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ valued in $[0,\infty]$ and a mesh $h > 0$, the *discrete kernel* is the cube average
--   $$\omega^k_h(x,y) \;=\; h^{-2d} \iint_{A_h(x) \times A_h(y)} k(s,t)\, d(s,t)$$
--   (written `discreteKernel d k h x y`). Let $\Gamma$ be a configuration on $\mathbb{R}^d$, assigning to each point a double cone with carrier $(\Gamma x).\mathrm{carrier}$, and assume:
--
--   - $\mathrm{KernelBounds}(\Gamma, \alpha, \Lambda, k)$: $1 \le \Lambda$, $k$ is symmetric, and for all $x,y$
--   $$\Lambda^{-1}\bigl(\mathbf 1_{V^\Gamma[x]}(y) + \mathbf 1_{V^\Gamma[y]}(x)\bigr)\lVert x-y\rVert^{-d-\alpha} \;\le\; k(x,y) \;\le\; \Lambda \lVert x - y\rVert^{-d-\alpha},$$
--   where $V^\Gamma[x] = x + (\Gamma x).\mathrm{carrier}$; this is assumption (2) of Theorem 1.1;
--   - $\alpha > 0$;
--   - $F$ is a family of reference cones for $\Gamma$ of aperture $\theta$ (a `RefFamily Γ θ`): a finite set $F.\mathrm{axes}$ of unit vectors such that every $(\Gamma x).\mathrm{carrier}$ contains $V(v,\theta)$ for some $v \in F.\mathrm{axes}$, where $V(v,\theta)$ is the double cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\theta$. For a vector $m$ one writes $F.\mathrm{cone}\,m = V(m,\theta)$, and
--   $$F.\mathrm{shrunkAt}\,m\,\rho\,x \;=\; x + \bigl(V(m,\theta)\bigr)_{\rho}, \qquad (S)_\rho = \{u \in S : \bar B(u,\rho) \subseteq S\},$$
--   the $\rho$-shrinking of that reference cone, translated to have apex at $x$;
--   - $\mathrm{CondMeas}(\Gamma)$: for every set $V$, the set $\{x : V \subseteq (\Gamma x).\mathrm{carrier}\}$ is measurable;
--   - $h > 0$, and $x, y \in h\mathbb{Z}^d$ with the separation $\sqrt{d}\,h < \|x - y\|$.
--
--   Then for **arbitrary** $m, n \in \mathbb{R}^d$, writing $\mathbf{1}_A$ for the $[0,\infty]$-valued indicator of a set $A$, $|\cdot|$ for Lebesgue measure and
--   $$A_h^{V}(u) \;=\; \{ s \in A_h(u) : V \subseteq (\Gamma s).\mathrm{carrier}\}$$
--   for the cone-restricted cube,
--   $$h^{-2d}\,\Lambda^{-1}\Big(\mathbf{1}_{F.\mathrm{shrunkAt}\,m\,(h\sqrt{d})\,x}(y) + \mathbf{1}_{F.\mathrm{shrunkAt}\,n\,(h\sqrt{d})\,y}(x)\Big)\,(2\sqrt{d})^{-d-\alpha}\,\|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}\ \big|A_h^{V(m,\theta)}(x)\big|\ \big|A_h^{V(n,\theta)}(y)\big| \;\le\; \omega^k_h(x,y).$$
--
--   This is the lower half of the discrete kernel bounds at mesh $h$: it converts the pointwise lower bound on $k$ over a pair of well-separated cubes into a bound on the averaged kernel, the price being the measures of the two cone-restricted sub-cubes, which measure how much of each tile actually sees the relevant reference cone. The factor $(2\sqrt{d})^{-d-\alpha}$ is the loss incurred by replacing $\|s-t\|$ for $s,t$ in the two cubes by $\|x - y\|$, and the prefactor $h^{-2d}$ is the normalization of the average. Because everything is scale-covariant, the constants are the same as at mesh $1$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** All quantities live in $\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{\infty}$; the real-valued factors are injected by `ENNReal.ofReal`, so a negative real would be sent to $0$ rather than causing an error. The indices $m$ and $n$ are universally quantified with **no** hypothesis placing them in $F.\mathrm{axes}$ or even normalizing them: since $F.\mathrm{cone}\,m = V(m,\theta)$ makes no reference to $F$, the statement is really about the double cone about an arbitrary vector. For such an $m$ the corresponding indicator is the one that degenerates — e.g. for $m = 0$ the cone $V(0,\theta)$ is empty, hence so is its shrinking and its translate, and the left-hand side collapses to $0$, making the inequality trivially true. The measurability hypothesis $\mathrm{CondMeas}(\Gamma)$ is carried explicitly because the measurable-selection argument that would supply it is not formalized here.
